9b50a83847d2a5dfbb9c4c81c4c457a2cd467.png)\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n\n- $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n\n- $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/example-6996.json" "file_sha256" "780da8bd7ef315d02084244a16d8c10280648e0aa98efe78bf15346c51028ea9"} {"question" "e-6996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/8e0d0d508d61352edf461f147cc561a527f6b3191e6c6b11d070eedf1e6572e3.png)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/example-6996.json" "file_sha256" "780da8bd7ef315d02084244a16d8c10280648e0aa98efe78bf15346c51028ea9"}] "usage" {"q-80266" [] "e-6996" []} "basis" 1741 "retractions" [[17592186152745 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} = 1$. If\n$f(x, y) = {xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖Dand∬_(D)xydA = ∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R$?\n\n1. $R = [- 2, 2] \\cdot [- 1, 1]$\n2. $R = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [- 2, 2]$\n3. $R = [- 2, 2] \\cdot [- 2, 2]$"] [17592186151933 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ shown above. If\n${x^{2} - y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D$\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2616/Source/Images/f62ea4c35fcc7d18eac85d6d253ef727.png)\n\n1. $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n2. $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n3. $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$"] [17592186151933 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define\n\n$$\n{x^{2} - y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D\n$$\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186151381 17592186151382 17592186151383 17592186151384 17592186151385] "immutable_fields" [[17592186151380 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151380 :answer-field/id] [17592186151380 :answer-field/key] [17592186151380 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
